kosha aromatic

  -- benzene ring and its derivatives. 4n+2 π electrons (Hückel rule). special stability.
  -- C6H6: 6 carbons in a ring, alternating single/double bonds — but actually
  -- fully delocalised: each carbon contributes one p-electron to a π cloud above
  -- and below the ring. no single bond, no double bond — something in between.
  -- this delocalisation IS the aromaticity. it makes benzene far more stable
  -- than a simple triene (cyclohexatriene) would be.
  --
  -- reactions: electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS) — NOT addition.
  -- adding to the ring would break aromaticity. substitution preserves it.
  -- nitration, halogenation, sulfonation, Friedel-Crafts (alkylation/acylation).
  --
  -- benzene: C6H6. carcinogenic. solvent. precursor to almost all aromatics.
  -- toluene: C6H5-CH3. less toxic. paint thinner. TNT precursor.
  -- phenol: C6H5-OH. antiseptic. phenolic resins. aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid.
  -- aniline: C6H5-NH2. dye industry. rubber accelerators.
  --
  -- biological aromatics: amino acids (Phe, Tyr, Trp), DNA bases, heme, chlorophyll.
  -- pi-pi stacking: aromatic rings stack face-to-face — drives protein folding, DNA.

